# Project One Move-Between-Rooms Pseudocode
#
# Graded checkpoint: Module Five Project One
# Later use: conceptual support for M6; movement design for M7
#
# Review the course-provided movement flowchart before completing this file.
# Write pseudocode, not Python code. Use clear indentation and course-appropriate
# keywords such as INPUT, OUTPUT, IF, ELSE, WHILE, SET, or similar terms.


# TODO 1: Identify the information the movement process needs.
# The program must know the player's current room and the available directions.
SET current_room TO player's starting room
SET valid_directions TO ["north", "south", "east", "west"]

# TODO 2: Prompt for and receive the player's movement command.
OUTPUT "Enter a direction to move (north, south, east, or west):"
INPUT player_direction
CONVERT player_direction TO lowercase

# TODO 3: Validate whether the requested movement is possible from the current room.
IF player_direction IS IN valid_directions THEN
IF current_room HAS a connected room IN player_direction THEN
# TODO 4: Describe what happens when the movement command is valid.
SET current_room TO connected room IN player_direction
OUTPUT "You move " + player_direction + " and enter the " + current_room + "."
ELSE
# TODO 5: Describe what happens when the movement command is invalid.
OUTPUT "You cannot go that way."
ENDIF
ELSE
OUTPUT "Invalid direction. Please enter north, south, east, or west."
ENDIF

# TODO 6: Show how movement fits into repeated gameplay.
# The game continues accepting movement commands until a required ending occurs.
WHILE game_is_active
REPEAT movement process
IF player HAS collected all items THEN
OUTPUT "You have all items! Proceed to the Containment Unit to win."
SET game_is_active TO FALSE
ELSE IF player ENTERS villain_room THEN
OUTPUT "Specimen X attacks! Game over."
SET game_is_active TO FALSE
ENDIF
ENDWHILE
